From: Escapement
Subject: [PATCH] config: enable the RTC class and the PCF85063A

The shipped config has no RTC support of any kind -- no CONFIG_RTC_CLASS, so
/dev/rtc* does not exist and hwclock has nothing to talk to. This adds the
minimum needed to read the watch's PCF85063A: the RTC class, the driver, and
hctosys so the kernel seeds its own clock at boot instead of starting at the
epoch every time.

Modules are off in this config (no CONFIG_MODULES), so these are =y. The
kernel is XIP from flash and module support would cost RAM this board does
not have to spare.

CONFIG_RTC_HCTOSYS is the point of the exercise. Without it the driver is
just a readable file and userspace still has to run hwclock; with it the
kernel sets the wall clock from the RTC before init starts, which is what
makes timestamps, TLS certificate checks and jffs2 mtimes correct on a board
that has no NTP until WiFi associates.

Deliberately not included: alarms, the UIE/PIE/AIE interfaces, and
CONFIG_RTC_DRV_AXP2101 -- this board's clock is the PCF85063A, not the PMU's.
Those are a later pass.

--- a/new-files/board/espressif/esp32s3/devkit_c1_16m_linux.config
+++ b/new-files/board/espressif/esp32s3/devkit_c1_16m_linux.config
@@ -110,6 +110,14 @@
 CONFIG_I2C=y
 CONFIG_I2C_CHARDEV=y
 CONFIG_I2C_GPIO=y
+# The watch has a PCF85063A on I2C 0x51, backed by VBACKUP, and it is the only
+# clock on the board that survives power loss. hctosys is what makes it useful:
+# it seeds the kernel clock before init, so timestamps and certificate checks
+# are right without waiting for NTP.
+CONFIG_RTC_CLASS=y
+CONFIG_RTC_HCTOSYS=y
+CONFIG_RTC_HCTOSYS_DEVICE="rtc0"
+CONFIG_RTC_DRV_PCF85063=y
 CONFIG_SPI=y
 CONFIG_SPI_ESP32=y
 CONFIG_PINCTRL=y
